# Breaking and Continuing Loops in Twig Templates

Twig does not support `break` or `continue` statements directly within its template syntax. This limitation stems from Twig's design philosophy: it is a logic-less language intended for presentation, deliberately stripping away imperative control structures to keep templates simple and predictable. While PHP allows you to jump out of loops or skip iterations using these keywords, attempting to use them inside a `{% %}` block in Twig will result in a syntax error.

If your requirement is to stop rendering content after a specific condition is met, the native solution involves restructuring the loop logic to handle the termination state explicitly. You can achieve this by introducing an intermediate variable that acts as a flag, indicating whether the loop should proceed with rendering subsequent items.

Consider the scenario where you want to display posts until you encounter the one with an ID of 10. In PHP, you would simply use `break`. In Twig, you must manage the flow manually:

```twig
{% set stopRendering = false %}

{% for post in posts %}
    {% if post.id == 10 %}
        {% set stopRendering = true %}
    {% endif %}

    {% if not stopRendering %}
        <h2>{{ post.heading }}</h2>
        <!-- Render other content here -->
    {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
```

In this example, the logic mimics a `break` by setting a flag when the condition is met. The rendering block is guarded by an `if not stopRendering` check. This pattern forces the loop to iterate through all items but prevents any HTML output once the target item is reached.

While this approach works, it has a significant trade-off: the loop still processes every single item in the collection. If your list contains thousands of records and you only need the first few, iterating over the entire array can waste CPU cycles and memory. To address performance concerns with large datasets, you should filter the data at the application level before passing it to the view.

If you are using a modern PHP environment or a framework like Laravel, the most efficient strategy is to handle the filtering in the controller or service layer rather than inside the template. You can use `array_slice` or a generator-based approach to limit the iteration count:

```php
$posts = array_slice($posts, 0, $stopIndex);
```

By doing this in PHP, you ensure that Twig only receives the data it actually needs to render. This shifts the complexity away from the template and aligns with the separation of concerns principle. The controller calculates which items should be displayed, and Twig simply renders what is handed to it.

There are times, however, when you might need to skip an iteration without stopping the loop entirely, effectively replicating `continue`. Twig supports this natively using the `{% continue %}` statement within a `for` or `while` loop. If you have a list of posts and want to omit any that lack a title, you can write:

```twig
{% for post in posts %}
    {% if not post.title %}
        {% continue %}
    {% endif %}

    <h2>{{ post.heading }}</h2>
{% endfor %}
```

This behavior is fully supported and behaves similarly to PHP's `continue`. It skips the current iteration immediately, moving control flow back to the start of the loop for the next item.

Ultimately, while Twig lacks direct equivalents to every PHP control structure, it provides robust alternatives like flags for breaking loops and native support for continuing iterations. When performance becomes a bottleneck or when the logic grows too complex for simple templates, moving that logic back into your application code is often the cleanest and most maintainable solution. For deeper insights into building performant views in Laravel specifically, you might find additional context at <https://laravelcompany.com>.
